ts shown  as magenta circles in the movie that's  playing  create information in the form of  three-dimensional trajectories in time  so each of the lines that you see in  this what we call spaghetti plot each of  the the noodles if you will shows you  where the bound events occurred in time  and in space for this particular cell  and you can also see in the topmost  plane in shown in green the activating  information in the form of the n-fat  translocation event so i'll go ahead and  play that one more time  so given this time evolution we have all  kinds of information about this cell  experienced  we can take these kinds of data  and then try to understand  what led to making the activating  decision  and remarkably the simplest hypotheses  fail so the simplest hypotheses would be  that the t cell counted the number of  binding events it experienced  or that it summed up the total time of  binding that it experienced and that  integrated binding time set the  activation threshold  in fact neither of these is the case and  rather what we found in the course of  this work is that t cells are paying  attention to both where and when binding  events occur  so keep in mind that more often than not  binding events are very short because  real agonist ligands have low affinities  that are very almost indistinguishable  from self ligands and so in fact what  you can see in the schematic on the left  is if you were to imagine one bound  trajectory shown in blue that is  experienced for some amount of time and  then it disengages and another binding  event shown in red that over some sort  of temporal window follows the blue  event and it's also somewhat nearby in  space what we call the spatial  neighborhood  in fact what appears to happen is the t  cell artificially stitches these two  events together  and interprets it as one very long  binding event and from a biochemical  perspective there's a good intuition  here that the longer the binding event  the greater the amplitude of signal  because the longer time there is for  biochemical reactions to occur and so  quite remarkably we were able to show  that in fact what a t cell does in  making an activating decision is use  these multiply correlated tracts so what  you see here in this simplified  schematic is the idea that shown in gray  boxes are the amplitudes of signal  in the context of non-activating cells  we can see that short binding events  lead to low amplit or short small  amplitude signals whereas long binding  events or these binding events that are  spatio temporally correlated as you see  on the far right can sustain greater  signal and lead to activation  so again i want to emphasize a couple of  features about this impulse response  assay the first is that we can see both  activating and non-activating cells and  this is really important because it  allows us to distinguish threshold  activities for those cells that do make  a decision say to activate and those  that do not  and then finally even though there is  this spatiotemporal correlation it's  quite interesting that the scales of  these correlations are larger than we  might immediately predict  so events that are within about two  microns in space and 30 seconds in time  can be stitched together and i don't  have time to talk about the underlying  machinery but we also have newer data  hopefully in an upcoming publication  that show that this lat molecule that i  briefly mentioned earlier may be  mediating this spatiotemporal  correlation but ultimately what i want  to go on to is the discussion of what  does this mean in the context of  chimeric energy t cells and so this is  the work that we've been expanding since  i moved to purdue  where you can imagine that now by  knowing that t cells are capable of  spatiotemporal correlation we could  imagine the same might be true for  engineered t cells but if it's not we  would probably want to know this in  order to better engineer  robust responses moving for