ng quickly are  blurred and only those binding events  that are occurring on this time scale  are clearly resolved  so by taking advantage of that  information we can now individually  watch the binding events that are  encountered by a single living t cell  and this is important because it gives  us information about when a signaling  cascade may be kicked off so i revisit  this cartoon of the biochemistry of tcr  signaling to remind you that once you  have bound receptors a number of  downstream events are possible and a  cellular response is possible  but in order to try and answer the  fundamental question of what activates a  t cell we tried to simplify this idea  using an analog to signal processing so  we take the idea that this could be an  impulse response function and by  impulses i mean discrete pieces of  information that go into the system so  here our system is going to be the cell  and these impulses can be modulated  either in amplitude or in frequency that  is how strong an interaction might be a  binding event or how frequently they  might be encountered  and this black box is in fact all of the  cellular biochemistry that's happening  in the cell that is so complex and  difficult to measure but critical to the  transformation process that the cell  will then act on to create a response or  the input output response function  what's really beautiful about this  response is we can imagine that by  measuring the individual binding events  that a t cell experiences and without  measuring all of the intracellular  complex biochemistry  if we can determine that the cell did  something made a decision and in this  case we take the binary response of  translocation of a transcription factor  and fat into this  we now can determine the transformation  through  extraction rather than through direct  observation  so it's easiest to understand this by  actually seeing it in the context of a  movie and what you'll see in the movie i  will play is this ricm channel which  again shows us membrane topography so we  will see living t cells come and land on  the bilayer  and in the middle channel the pmhc ato  channel this is where the inputs are  being measured so each of the binding  events that the t cell experiences  and then finally on the right-hand  channel in the n-fat we can see the  translocation of the transcription  factor and fat into the nucleus and as i  mentioned this is a binary decision so  once the transcription factor goes into  the nucleus that cell is committed to  activating  and so we'll watch this process uh in  the movie and then i'll make a couple  comments after that  so here we see the cell land  and it spreads  and forms an interface that we see here  in ricm we can see the circled binding  events in red and then over time we'll  notice that the n-fat goes from the  cytoplasm into the nucleus and that  translocation demonstrates it  demonstrates that this cell has  activated  it may be a little easier to look at  these data in a more static sense so  here's a montage of that same movie and  i'll point out a couple of really  important features the first is that we  can directly visualize the process of  cell landing that precedes binding  events and the decision-making process  thereby having the complete history of  information that this cell experienced  so we know the moment it started  interpreting information from its local  environment what those information  looked like in terms of single molecule  binding events and ultimately whether or  not the cell activated  so all these pieces of information allow  us to have a timeline  as well as spatial information about all  of the impulses that went in as well as  their response and one could imagine  that by knowing the impulses in the  response we can get gather information  about the transformation  so here we can see all of that  information collapsed into a simple 3d  movie where the binding events are being  accumulated so the binding events shown  as magenta circles in the movie that's  playing  create information in the form of  three-dimensional trajectories in time  so each of the lines that you see in  this what we call spaghetti plot each of  the the noodles if you will shows you  where the bound events occurred in time  and in space for this particular cell  and you can also see in the topmost  plane in shown in green the activating  information in the form of the n-fat  translocation event so i'll go ahead and  play that one more time  so given this time evolution we have all  kinds of information about this cell  experienced  we can take these kinds of data  and then try to understand  what led to making the activating  decision  and remarkably the simplest hypotheses  fail so the simplest hypotheses would be  that the t cell counted the number of  binding events it experienced  or that it summed up the total time of  binding that it experienced and that  integrated binding time set the  activation threshold  in fact neither of these is the case and  rather what we found in the course of  this work is that t cells are paying  attention to both where and when binding  events occur  so keep in mind that more often than not  binding events are very short because  real agonist ligands have low affinities  that are very almost indistinguishable  from self ligands and so in fact what  you can see in the schematic on the left  is if you were to imagine one bound  trajectory shown in blue that is  experienced for some amount of time and  then it disengages and another binding  event shown in red that over some sort  of temporal window follows the blue  event and it's also somewhat nearby in  space what we call the spatial  neighborhood  in fact what appears to happen is the t  cell artificially stitches these two  events together  and interprets it as one very long  binding event and from a biochemical  perspective there's a good intuition  here that the longer the binding event  the greater the amplitude of signal  because the longer time there is for  biochemical reactions to occur and so  quite remarkably we were able to show  that in fact what a t cell does in  making an activating decision is use  these multiply correlated tracts so what  you see here in this simplified  schematic is the idea that shown in gray  boxes are the amplitudes of signal  in the context of non-activating cells  we can see that short binding events  lead to low amplit or short small  amplitude signals whereas long binding  events or these binding events that are  spatio temporally correlated as you see  on the far right can sustain greater  signal and lead to activation  so again i want to emphasize a couple of  features about this impulse response  assay the first is that we can see both  activating and non-activating cells and  this is really important because it  allows us to distinguish threshold  activities for those cells that do make  a decision say to activate and those  that do not  and then finally even though there is  this spatiotemporal correlation it's  quite interesting that the scales of  these correlations are larger than we  might immediately predict  so events that are within about two  microns in space and 30 seconds in time  can be stitched together and i don't  have time to talk about the underlying  machinery but we also have newer data  hopefully in an upcoming publication  that show that this lat molecule that i  briefly mentioned earlier may be  mediating this spatiotemporal  correlation but ultimately what i want  to go on to is the discussion of what  does this mean in the context of  chimeric energy t cells and so this is  the work that we've been expanding since  i moved to purdue  where you can imagine that now by  knowing that t cells are capable of  spatiotemporal correlation we could  imagine the same might be true for  engineered t cells but if it's not we  would probably want to know this in  order to better engineer  robust responses moving forward  the idea here is that by having an  artificially engineered receptor that  can recognize a tumor cell you could  create an activation cascade within a t  cell from a patient that then leads to  one of two types of activation either  mobilizing adaptive responses from other  cells or perhaps directly killing the  target tumor cell  and so we still ask the underlying  question what are the molecular features  that control this activation  and it'd be tempting to think that these  chimeric inogen receptor t cells signal  exactly like a tcr but in fact we have  plenty of evidence that they are quite  different the first is that that  canonical structure that i talked about  that allows for concentration of bound  tcr's at the center and other binding  events of larger physical dimension like  adhesion  that creates this structure called the  immunological synapse that you see in  the overlay where there are very tight  tcrs in the center and then icam bound  adhesion complexes uh in red outside  there there's plenty of evidence that in  a chimeric antigen receptor t cell  context this very well known structure  of the immunological synapse does not  exist  and so already there are good reasons to  think that cars signal differently and  that we need to better understand how  they are signaling  so in partnership with phil loudlab here  at purdue we've been taking advantage of  an engineering strategy that phil's lab  has developed  and so this takes advantage of a  slightly different strategy that is  called a unicar so rather than a car  alone this universal strategy is based  on having cars that recognize  a single molecule fitzy which is not one  that you would see physiologically but  that by having that fizzy molecule on a  bridge here we show fitzy folate  then the folate or the folic acid binds  to the tumor itself and bridges between  the tumor and the car this carries the  the benefit that you could then have a  single population of cars that could be  targeted to many different cancers  simply by modifying the bridge  the other convenient thing is that since  fitzy is a fluorescent molecule it can  be used in laser-guided surgery because  as you can see toward the upper right by  having the fitsy folate within a tumor  you can see it glow and light up which  may also help in tumor resections  but with phil's lab what we're trying to  do is to understand  how a unicar actually a universal car  actually activates so what my student  has been doing  is creating bi-layer systems that have  the folate receptor on it displayed at  different densities and then using the  bridging multifolate and the anti-fitzy  car to better understand the activation  thresholds in several different  parameter spaces the effects of affinity  of both the car itself and of the bridge  the densities of the molecules which  could be the densities of the car  receptors on the surface the density of  the folate receptor  and then copy numbers and clustering so  of course as i mentioned very early on  small numbers of signaling molecules may  have large effects and so we're trying  to understand  using this reconstitution approach how  all of these different parameters fit  into the activation decision for a car  and you can see our impulse response  function  exemplified here where again we assume  that much of the biochemical signaling  is as it normally would be in a t cell  receptor system but have good reason to  think that there may be additional  pathways  that are in play because unlike a tcr a  chimeric engine receptor t cell is only  a single past transmembrane receptor so  it's fundamentally quite different than  a tcr  but our impulses of binding events  between  the car and the target folate  molecule on the folate receptor can then  lead to activation decisions in at least  two forms that we're thinking of right  now one is transcription factor  translocation as we've seen before in  other t-cell contexts as well as the  secretion of lytic molecules like  perforin and granzyme so these are  actually the prototypical molecules  secreted to actually kill target tumor  cells  and we're working to better understand  this impulse response function uh in the  context of breast cancer  another development that kevin has been  working on  is to try and increase the sampling of  the events that we are seeing so one  challenge that we faced in the early  development of the impulse response  function is the need to to capture the  but the landing and spreading of t cells  and i mentioned how powerful this  information is because it sets the full  timeline for that t cell but of course  it can be difficult to capture this  event  so kevin is also working on strategies  to increase the number of events that we  can visualize simultaneously through  larger fields of view and so you here  can see  t cells coming in and landing on a  bi-layer and you can beautifully catch  at least a few um maybe up to 10 or more  binding events  landing and spreading events for t cells  at the same time  as we try to directly look for single  molecule carb binding events as you'll  see in the next two movies we can watch  these very dynamic car t cells  using our reflection interference  contrast channel and we're getting very  close to having really beautiful data of  single molecule binding so i'll play  that in the next two movies  so here you can see a car on a bi-layer  and you can see it's membrane  fluctuations as it samples the  information around it just really  beautiful  t-cell interactions with the slb  and in this video you can start to see  binding events as these really bright  spots that are coalescing underneath  that t cell that we had just been  visualizing and so clear evidence that  these binding events are being  accumulated  so moving forward  we are now able to see these binding  events between the folate receptor and  the cars and where this is occurring in  time and space and this is giving us the  ability to start looking at these  impulse response functions and complex  signaling circuits in search of the key  thresholds for activation so that we can  better design cars and these signaling  circuits to maximize killing efficiency  but you might ask the question how does  this then translate back to the  three-dimensional context because of  course reconstitution does lack some of  the information that would occur at a  true cell cell junction  so in parallel we are also developing  systems that allow us to control  this 3d junction in such a way that we  can use living tumor cells and living  cars  where we now know the densities of  receptors on the surface of the tumor  and can look at the killing efficiency  by the cars directly thereby testing  some of the theories and models that  come out of our reconstitution methods  so hopefully a lot of exciting things  are on the horizon  my student has been doing an absolutely  fantastic job building up these systems  and we really are poised to measure the  impulse response function across many  orders of magnitude for many cells to  better understand how these cars are  being activated and what the threshold  criteria are  so with that  i'll thank the members of my group so  this is the current group  kevin being my main student ari another  graduate student through a collaboration  and our three undergrads and i often  tease my students that one of the skills  we have to learn in science is about  juggling and i'm really pleased uh that  they're all tackling this challenge as  difficult as it can be at times and  really doing a fantastic job trying to  become really great interdisciplinary  scientists  and finally it's really important to  also acknowledge the wonderful  collaborators and mentors that i've had  and continue to work with so jay groves  at uc berkeley i was with him when we  developed the impulse response function  and now we're getting to apply it here  at purdue with phil lao  and we have a host of other really  fantastic collaborators and many fun and  exciting things on the horizon  so thank you all for your attention  thanks again to the organizers for the  efforts and the invitation and i'll look  forward to future interactions and  questions
 

